Q: How can I combine these functions for different elements into one function? A: <p>Let's add an alternative: use a factory function to create a small object containing all you need to 'fly' a box (<a href="https://depth-first.com/articles/2019/03/04/class-free-object-oriented-programming/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">class free object oriented</a>).</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="false" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>flyBoxFactory("box1").fly();
flyBoxFactory("box2").fly();
flyBoxFactory("box3").fly();

function flyBoxFactory(boxId, distance) {
  const box = document.querySelector(`#${boxId}`);
  const initialLeft = box.getBoundingClientRect().left;
  let left = initialLeft, reverted = false;
  const maxLeft = initialLeft + (distance || 300);

  return Object.freeze({ fly });

  function move(revert) { 
    left += revert ? -1 : 1;
    box.style.left = `${left}px`;
  }

  function fly() {
    switch(true) {
      case left === initialLeft: reverted = false;
      case left &lt; maxLeft &amp;&amp; !reverted: move(reverted); break;
      case left &gt;= maxLeft || reverted: move(reverted = true);
    }

    return setTimeout(fly);
  }
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.box {
  position: absolute; 
  left: 10px; 
  width: 50px; 
  height: 50px; 

  &amp;.b1 {
    top: 10px; 
    background-color: #aa39fc;
  }

  &amp;.b2 {
    top: 65px;
    background-color: #2c79f1;
  }

  &amp;.b3 {
    top: 120px;
    left: 60px;
    background-color: green;
  }
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div id="box1" class="box b1"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div id="box2" class="box b2"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div id="box3" class="box b3"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: How can I get an unordered sequence of 2 elements by using choice in xsd 1.0? A: <p>I think your schema processor is giving you a misleading error message. The real error here is that the content model is ambiguous: if the first thing is an <code>&lt;estado&gt;</code> element, then you don't know which branch to take.</p>
<p>I'm guessing you want a content model that allows A, AB, or BA (but not B) where A is <code>estado</code> and B is <code>extra</code>. The way to write that is as (AB? | BA), that is</p>
<pre><code>        &lt;xs:choice&gt;
            &lt;xs:sequence&gt;
            &lt;xs:element name=&quot;estado&quot; maxOccurs=&quot;unbounded&quot; minOccurs=&quot;1&quot;&gt;&lt;/xs:element&gt;
            &lt;xs:element name=&quot;extra&quot; maxOccurs=&quot;unbounded&quot; minOccurs=&quot;0&quot;&gt;&lt;/xs:element&gt;                                                                                  
            &lt;/xs:sequence&gt;

            &lt;xs:sequence&gt;
            &lt;xs:element name=&quot;extra&quot; maxOccurs=&quot;unbounded&quot; minOccurs=&quot;1&quot;&gt;&lt;/xs:element&gt;                                                                                                                  
            &lt;xs:element name=&quot;estado&quot; maxOccurs=&quot;unbounded&quot; minOccurs=&quot;1&quot;&gt;&lt;/xs:element&gt;
            &lt;/xs:sequence&gt;

        &lt;/xs:choice&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>The answer from @ChrisMaden looks wrong to me. It's quite possible to have two occurrences of &quot;extra&quot; or of &quot;estado&quot; in your content model, provided that they have the same type.</p>

Q: How to decode this mojibaked CSV using PHP? A: <p>probably you're dealing with double-encoded UTF-8 mojibake where the data was originally UTF-8, misread as Windows-1252, then saved again</p>
<p><code>mb_convert_encoding()</code> fails because the conversion direction is reversed</p>
<p>so you must decode UTF-8 back into Windows-1252 repeatedly until it stabilizes.</p>
<pre><code>function fixMojibake(string $s): string
{
    for ($i = 0; $i &lt; 3; $i++) {
        if (!preg_match('/Ã.|â.|Â./', $s)) {
            break;
        }
        $s = mb_convert_encoding($s, 'Windows-1252', 'UTF-8');
    }
    return $s;
}

</code></pre>
<p>this should because mojibake is reversible, each pass removes one layer of corruption</p>

Q: How to center an element in the middle of the browser window? A: <p>I had a lot of problems with centring and alignment until I found Flexbox as a recommendation in a guide.</p>
<p><a href="http://css-tricks.com/snippets/css/a-guide-to-flexbox/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">A Complete Guide to Flexbox</a></p>
<p>I'll post a snippet (that works with Chrome) here for convenience.</p>
<p>For more details, please refer to the article.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>html {
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
}

body {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>This is text!</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
